					],
					"documentation": "Scope: one update cycle for one hall. A new sensor reading or an authorised staff change starts another cycle. Monitoring continues across all 40 halls; the final node only ends this update cycle.\n\nPreconditions: a valid latest count, safe capacity greater than zero, configured humidity limit and normal admission rate for sensitive halls, and an authenticated authorised staff change when the staff path is used. Device/communication failure recovery is outside this business workflow.\n\nUpdate source is an alternative, not parallel work: a sensor update supplies an anonymous count every minute and a humidity reading for a sensitive hall. A staff update supplies a permitted closure/reopening or conservation-clearance change. A staff update uses the latest stored count; it does not require a simultaneous sensor update. No staff action is required for each minute's sensor cycle.\n\nDocumented permissions: Security Supervisor closes/reopens halls for maintenance or VIP visits; Conservation sets safe capacity, and only Chief Curator may change it. Capacity configuration is read here, not performed in each cycle. Conservation clearance of a humidity hold is team assumption A-09.\n\nBefore deciding admission, update the humidity hold. Under A-08/A-09, above-limit humidity activates it. Normal readings alone do not clear an existing hold: authorised Conservation clearance is also required. A clearance request while humidity is above its limit cannot remove the hold. Updating this state even when the hall is closed prevents later reopening from discarding an uncleared restriction.\n\nClosed or full means an active authorised closure OR count >= safe capacity. Either stops new admissions. Otherwise an active humidity hold limits admissions per minute to 50% of the configured normal rate, rounded down. Safe capacity is unchanged, and remaining safe space always limits admission further. Otherwise allow/resume entry. Exits remain available. The action records the chosen entry status/reason. A falling count cannot override a manual closure or an uncleared humidity hold.\n\nThe warning decision executes after every entry outcome. Count >= 85% of capacity activates/maintains a crowd warning and alerts security, including when admissions are already stopped. Below 85%, clear only the crowd warning, leaving entry restrictions untouched. Notification throttling is a later implementation detail, not a staff-acknowledgement prerequisite.\n\nAfter the warning alternatives merge, a fork starts two independent automatic updates: visitor routes/guide availability and the live security dashboard. Routes exclude closed/full halls and suggest quieter halls at 85% or above. Routing respects conservation restrictions. The dashboard shows count, capacity, entry state/reason and warning. A join completes this cycle after both updates. No human acknowledgement is required. This parallel delivery is a modelling choice; it does not delay the admission decision above it.\n\nSources: 00 Project Request (30-minute tickets are a different process; here: 85% routing, capacity stop, manual closures, minute count storage, hall status and guide updates); 03 Cameras for Counting email (final sponsor decision: anonymous counts, no facial recognition); 04 Artifact Protection and Hall Capacity Rules (capacity authority; humidity requires 50% entry reduction); 05 Technical Notes (40 halls, counts each minute); 07 Crowd Crush Incident Report (85% alert, automatic admission stop, security live view).\n\nTeam assumptions used: A-08 (humidity sensors and admissions-per-minute interpretation), A-09 (clearance and independent restrictions), A-10 (security dashboard audience). They are not represented as additional statements from the museum documents.\n",
